1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,970
Maybe you know this feeling.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:06,310
You come home from work in the
evening. And you are tired.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,490
You don't really feel like
doing anything complicated.

4
00:00:10,742 --> 00:00:12,352
So you turn on your computer.

5
00:00:12,582 --> 00:00:14,412
And you start a game.

6
00:00:15,082 --> 00:00:17,212
Not because you desperately want to play.

7
00:00:17,562 --> 00:00:19,652
It's just the easiest thing to do.

8
00:00:20,423 --> 00:00:21,813
You already know the game.

9
00:00:22,503 --> 00:00:24,003
You know how everything works.

10
00:00:24,083 --> 00:00:25,813
You don't have to learn anything new.

11
00:00:26,263 --> 00:00:28,353
You don't have to figure out what to play.

12
00:00:29,077 --> 00:00:34,397
You just log in. And then you start doing
things. Daily quests. Collecting rewards.

13
00:00:34,477 --> 00:00:37,227
Levelling your character.
Upgrading your equipment.

14
00:00:38,093 --> 00:00:40,873
Farming resources.
Completing achievements.

15
00:00:40,953 --> 00:00:44,103
And, most importantly,
moving progress bars.

16
00:00:44,996 --> 00:00:47,126
And suddenly, three hours have passed.

17
00:00:47,856 --> 00:00:50,226
And you ask yourself:
Did I actually have fun?

18
00:00:51,025 --> 00:00:52,495
Maybe. Maybe not.

19
00:00:53,225 --> 00:00:55,605
Because sometimes you realise
that you spent the entire

20
00:00:55,685 --> 00:00:58,515
evening doing things you
didn't really want to do.

21
00:00:59,229 --> 00:01:01,119
You did them because they were there.

22
00:01:01,429 --> 00:01:03,019
Because they were unfinished.

23
00:01:03,649 --> 00:01:05,839
Because they were part of your routine.

24
00:01:06,229 --> 00:01:09,379
Because if you didn't do them
today, you would lose something.

25
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:13,590
And this raises an interesting
question: Why do we keep playing

26
00:01:13,670 --> 00:01:17,180
games even when we're no longer
enjoying the actual playing?

27
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:24,990
Traditional games have a fairly
simple structure. You start the game.

28
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,230
You play through it. And
eventually, you finish it.

29
00:01:28,877 --> 00:01:32,517
There is an ending. You can play
it again if you want, but there

30
00:01:32,597 --> 00:01:35,087
is still a point where
you can say: I'm done.

31
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:38,097
Online games can work very differently.

32
00:01:38,947 --> 00:01:40,537
They don't really have an ending.

33
00:01:41,027 --> 00:01:44,417
Instead, they give you an
enormous number of smaller goals.

34
00:01:45,353 --> 00:01:49,813
Levels. Gear. Collections. Achievements.
Skills. Reputation. Ranks. Quests.

35
00:01:49,893 --> 00:01:51,093
Seasonal rewards.

36
00:01:51,849 --> 00:01:55,589
And when you finish one thing, there
is another one waiting. And another.

37
00:01:55,669 --> 00:01:58,119
And another. You never
really reach the end.

38
00:02:00,759 --> 00:02:03,089
And there's nothing
inherently wrong with that.

39
00:02:03,779 --> 00:02:06,269
Progress is a fantastic game mechanic.

40
00:02:06,679 --> 00:02:10,189
Starting at zero and gradually
getting better feels good.

41
00:02:10,928 --> 00:02:12,938
Getting a new ability feels good.

42
00:02:13,588 --> 00:02:15,658
Completing a collection feels good.

43
00:02:16,408 --> 00:02:19,918
Finally defeating a boss you
couldn't defeat before feels good.

44
00:02:20,607 --> 00:02:22,477
The problem begins somewhere else.

45
00:02:23,187 --> 00:02:26,557
It begins when you stop doing
something because you enjoy doing it...

46
00:02:26,687 --> 00:02:29,197
and start doing it
because it's unfinished.

47
00:02:32,057 --> 00:02:34,877
Imagine a progress bar at
seventy-three per cent.

48
00:02:34,957 --> 00:02:37,607
What does that number actually mean?

49
00:02:37,837 --> 00:02:39,567
Nothing particularly important.

50
00:02:40,517 --> 00:02:44,607
But your brain immediately sees:
Twenty-seven per cent is missing.

51
00:02:45,337 --> 00:02:47,387
And suddenly there is something to fix.

52
00:02:48,317 --> 00:02:51,030
You play a little more.
Seventy-three becomes eighty-two.

53
00:02:51,110 --> 00:02:52,817
Eighty-two becomes ninety-four.

54
00:02:52,897 --> 00:02:55,807
And finally: One hundred per cent. Done.

55
00:02:56,583 --> 00:03:00,443
And that feels good. But if the
game is designed around permanent

56
00:03:00,523 --> 00:03:03,273
progression, something
else happens immediately.

57
00:03:04,212 --> 00:03:06,752
Another progress bar appears. Empty.

58
00:03:06,832 --> 00:03:08,012
And behind it, more.

59
00:03:08,092 --> 00:03:09,962
And the whole thing starts again.

60
00:03:12,632 --> 00:03:15,692
We often talk about rewards.
Experience points. Loot.

61
00:03:15,772 --> 00:03:18,082
Virtual currency. Cosmetics.

62
00:03:18,829 --> 00:03:21,779
But a reward is only one of
the things pulling at you.

63
00:03:22,149 --> 00:03:26,119
There is something quieter sitting
next to it: unfinished things.

64
00:03:27,013 --> 00:03:29,863
When you leave something
half-done, it often pulls you back.

65
00:03:30,713 --> 00:03:33,583
An unfinished task at
work. An unread message.

66
00:03:33,753 --> 00:03:35,363
A half-finished project.

67
00:03:36,135 --> 00:03:38,285
On its own, that pull is fairly weak.

68
00:03:38,855 --> 00:03:41,965
You put down a half-read book
and the world carries on.

69
00:03:42,435 --> 00:03:44,685
A game doesn't leave it on its own.

70
00:03:45,365 --> 00:03:47,815
It puts the unfinished
thing next to a reward.

71
00:03:48,025 --> 00:03:49,575
Next to a deadline.

72
00:03:49,925 --> 00:03:52,535
Next to a routine you already have.

73
00:03:52,725 --> 00:03:54,495
And then it shows you a number.

74
00:03:57,265 --> 00:03:59,415
It doesn't have to tell you: Keep playing.

75
00:03:59,985 --> 00:04:02,505
It can simply show you:
Eighty-seven per cent.

76
00:04:02,585 --> 00:04:04,835
And suddenly you want
to make it one hundred.

77
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:08,240
This creates a strange situation.

78
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:11,130
You stop asking: What
do I want to do tonight?

79
00:04:11,210 --> 00:04:14,600
And start asking: What am
I supposed to do tonight?

80
00:04:15,573 --> 00:04:17,603
That's a very important difference.

81
00:04:18,273 --> 00:04:22,123
Because now you're not necessarily
playing the game. You're maintaining it.

82
00:04:24,683 --> 00:04:26,153
You're doing your dailies.

83
00:04:26,443 --> 00:04:28,353
You're keeping up with the current meta.

84
00:04:28,643 --> 00:04:30,693
You're maintaining your character.

85
00:04:30,803 --> 00:04:32,193
You're collecting resources.

86
00:04:33,274 --> 00:04:35,464
You're making sure you don't fall behind.

87
00:04:36,194 --> 00:04:39,164
The game has become something
you have to take care of.

88
00:04:41,564 --> 00:04:46,054
But I want to finish everything.
Of course you do. That's the trap.

89
00:04:46,764 --> 00:04:50,114
Some games are built around goals
that can never really be finished.

90
00:04:50,965 --> 00:04:53,395
You can collect everything
that exists today.

91
00:04:54,125 --> 00:04:58,585
And tomorrow there is a new update. New
items. New achievements. New levels.

92
00:04:58,665 --> 00:05:00,395
New systems. New rewards.

93
00:05:01,239 --> 00:05:04,819
So if your goal is one hundred per
cent completion, the game can give you

94
00:05:04,899 --> 00:05:08,759
an impossible task: Finish everything
in a world that keeps expanding.

95
00:05:08,839 --> 00:05:10,629
And you keep trying.

96
00:05:13,729 --> 00:05:15,339
So how do you actually stop?

97
00:05:16,009 --> 00:05:19,189
The obvious answer is:
Just use more self-control.

98
00:05:19,769 --> 00:05:21,619
I don't think that's very good advice.

99
00:05:22,533 --> 00:05:24,813
Because when you're
tired after work, you're

100
00:05:24,893 --> 00:05:27,403
already dealing with the
worst possible combination.

101
00:05:28,453 --> 00:05:30,723
Low energy. A familiar habit.

102
00:05:30,833 --> 00:05:33,603
And a game that gives
you an immediate reward.

103
00:05:34,461 --> 00:05:36,361
Telling yourself: I'm not
going to play tonight.

104
00:05:36,441 --> 00:05:38,791
can be surprisingly difficult.

105
00:05:39,762 --> 00:05:42,502
So instead of relying
entirely on willpower,

106
00:05:42,582 --> 00:05:45,032
you can change the
environment around the habit.

107
00:05:47,552 --> 00:05:51,842
The important moment isn't necessarily
two hours into the gaming session.

108
00:05:52,852 --> 00:05:55,742
It's the moment when you
decide what to do after work.

109
00:05:56,503 --> 00:06:01,063
If your routine is: come home, sit
at the computer, launch the game,

110
00:06:01,143 --> 00:06:03,913
then you are already playing before
you've really made a decision.

111
00:06:04,791 --> 00:06:08,331
So make the first step less
automatic. Remove the shortcut.

112
00:06:08,411 --> 00:06:11,221
Turn off notifications. Log out.

113
00:06:12,013 --> 00:06:15,863
Don't let the game constantly remind
you that something is waiting for you.

114
00:06:16,533 --> 00:06:20,283
And, most importantly, prepare
alternatives. Not one alternative.

115
00:06:20,893 --> 00:06:22,093
Several.

116
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:25,840
A game you've already played.
A short game. A book. A movie.

117
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:27,610
Something you've been meaning to do.

118
00:06:28,335 --> 00:06:30,745
The goal isn't to find
a new perfect hobby.

119
00:06:31,615 --> 00:06:33,255
The goal is simply to make sure that the

120
00:06:33,335 --> 00:06:35,985
familiar game isn't the
only effortless option.

121
00:06:38,675 --> 00:06:41,375
If you really want to know how
much a game has taken over your

122
00:06:41,455 --> 00:06:45,305
routine, sometimes it helps to
stop completely for a while.

123
00:06:46,045 --> 00:06:50,365
Not as a punishment. Not as a
permanent decision. Just an experiment.

124
00:06:50,445 --> 00:06:51,405
Try two weeks.

125
00:06:51,485 --> 00:06:54,275
And pay attention to what happens.

126
00:06:55,169 --> 00:06:57,319
The first few days may feel strange.

127
00:06:58,189 --> 00:07:00,279
You may feel that something is missing.

128
00:07:00,889 --> 00:07:03,719
But eventually you may
discover something important:

129
00:07:04,656 --> 00:07:07,846
You don't actually miss the
game. You miss the routine.

130
00:07:08,736 --> 00:07:10,406
And those are not the same thing.

131
00:07:13,496 --> 00:07:15,436
Live-service games are
very good at creating the

132
00:07:15,516 --> 00:07:18,066
feeling that something
is happening right now.

133
00:07:18,744 --> 00:07:21,474
A reward expires. An event ends.

134
00:07:21,724 --> 00:07:23,754
A season is almost over.

135
00:07:24,004 --> 00:07:25,954
A daily bonus is waiting.

136
00:07:26,564 --> 00:07:28,734
If you don't log in today,
you'll miss something.

137
00:07:29,449 --> 00:07:31,849
But ask yourself: What
exactly am I going to lose?

138
00:07:31,929 --> 00:07:35,879
Some virtual currency?
An item? A daily reward?

139
00:07:36,369 --> 00:07:37,819
A position on a leaderboard?

140
00:07:38,571 --> 00:07:41,811
And what do I get in exchange
for not logging in? Time.

141
00:07:41,891 --> 00:07:43,301
Time to do something else.

142
00:07:44,128 --> 00:07:46,108
Because the real cost of these games isn't

143
00:07:46,188 --> 00:07:48,738
necessarily what you
spend inside the game.

144
00:07:49,408 --> 00:07:52,258
It may be what you don't
spend your time on outside it.

145
00:07:54,778 --> 00:07:56,528
Learn to leave things unfinished.

146
00:07:57,598 --> 00:07:59,328
This may be the hardest part.

147
00:07:59,938 --> 00:08:01,448
Leave the progress bar at
seventy-three per cent.

148
00:08:03,347 --> 00:08:05,037
Leave the achievement unfinished.

149
00:08:05,967 --> 00:08:07,557
Leave the collection incomplete.

150
00:08:08,227 --> 00:08:10,507
Leave the quest unfinished. Log out.

151
00:08:10,587 --> 00:08:11,937
And see what happens.

152
00:08:12,791 --> 00:08:14,961
Probably nothing. The world doesn't end.

153
00:08:15,471 --> 00:08:17,141
Your character doesn't disappear.

154
00:08:18,031 --> 00:08:20,541
The unfinished thing
simply remains unfinished.

155
00:08:20,751 --> 00:08:22,121
And you are somewhere else.

156
00:08:22,897 --> 00:08:26,917
If unfinished progress makes you
uncomfortable, one way to weaken that

157
00:08:26,997 --> 00:08:31,067
feeling is to sometimes let something
remain unfinished on purpose.

158
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:33,880
You don't have to win against the game.

159
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:37,340
You just have to prove to
yourself that you can walk away.

160
00:08:40,030 --> 00:08:43,220
Before you continue doing something
in the game, ask yourself:

161
00:08:43,790 --> 00:08:46,840
If there were no progress for
this, would I still want to do it?

162
00:08:47,529 --> 00:08:50,899
No experience points.
No loot. No achievement.

163
00:08:51,069 --> 00:08:52,729
No increase in a percentage.

164
00:08:52,809 --> 00:08:54,639
Would I still want to do it?

165
00:08:55,307 --> 00:08:57,607
If the answer is yes:
Great. You're playing.

166
00:08:57,687 --> 00:09:00,497
If the answer is no: Maybe
you're not playing anymore.

167
00:09:01,487 --> 00:09:02,877
Maybe you're just completing.

168
00:09:05,617 --> 00:09:07,747
None of this means that games are bad.

169
00:09:08,017 --> 00:09:10,707
And it doesn't mean that progress is bad.

170
00:09:11,237 --> 00:09:14,127
Progress is one of the
reasons games are fun.

171
00:09:14,847 --> 00:09:16,617
Collecting things can be fun.

172
00:09:17,047 --> 00:09:18,977
Getting better can be fun.

173
00:09:19,487 --> 00:09:22,677
Completing difficult goals
can be incredibly satisfying.

174
00:09:23,587 --> 00:09:26,057
The problem isn't: I
want to finish something.

175
00:09:26,447 --> 00:09:30,097
The problem is: I can't stop even
though I don't want to continue.

176
00:09:30,981 --> 00:09:33,351
That's the line worth paying attention to.

177
00:09:34,041 --> 00:09:36,611
And maybe the solution
isn't to stop playing games.

178
00:09:37,141 --> 00:09:39,671
Maybe it's to learn how
to play them differently.

179
00:09:42,261 --> 00:09:44,431
Sometimes you can play towards a goal.

180
00:09:45,261 --> 00:09:47,631
Sometimes you can chase
a difficult achievement.

181
00:09:48,701 --> 00:09:52,711
And sometimes you can simply wander around
and do something completely pointless.

182
00:09:53,711 --> 00:09:56,511
No progress. No optimisation. No reward.

183
00:09:56,591 --> 00:09:58,201
Just because it's fun.

184
00:09:58,969 --> 00:10:00,949
Because perhaps that's
something we forget when

185
00:10:01,029 --> 00:10:03,739
games become systems of
permanent progression.

186
00:10:04,749 --> 00:10:06,369
We forget what it feels like to play a

187
00:10:06,449 --> 00:10:08,679
game without having to
accomplish anything.

188
00:10:11,469 --> 00:10:13,129
You don't have to reach
one hundred per cent.

189
00:10:13,209 --> 00:10:15,599
You don't have to collect everything.

190
00:10:16,289 --> 00:10:18,439
You don't have to claim every reward.

191
00:10:19,269 --> 00:10:21,279
You don't have to finish every quest.

192
00:10:21,949 --> 00:10:24,059
You don't have to keep
up with every update.

193
00:10:24,849 --> 00:10:26,659
And you don't have to play every day.

194
00:10:27,475 --> 00:10:29,685
A game can offer you something valuable.

195
00:10:29,795 --> 00:10:33,405
But you don't have to give it
all of your free time in return.

196
00:10:34,379 --> 00:10:38,809
Because in the end, the most important
question isn't: What is left to complete?

197
00:10:39,599 --> 00:10:41,549
It's: What do I actually want to do?

198
00:10:44,099 --> 00:10:46,279
And sometimes the best
way to win a game...

199
00:10:46,359 --> 00:10:48,549
is simply to turn it off.
